### Origin versus site (get these two right first)

An **origin** is scheme + host + port. `http://localhost:9100` and `http://localhost:9101` are **different origins** (the port differs) although they are the **same site**. A **site** is roughly the scheme plus the registrable domain, and ports are ignored. `http://localhost:9101` and `http://127.0.0.1:9100` are different origins *and* different sites. The two terms matter because **CORS and the same-origin policy use origins, while `SameSite` cookies use sites**, which is why a CORS rule can block a read while a cookie is still sent.

### The same-origin policy and CORS

By default, JavaScript on one origin **cannot read** responses from another. The server can opt in with **CORS** (cross-origin resource sharing) response headers, chiefly `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`. The seven tests below fetch the API from the page. Read each pair of lines: what the page could do, and (in the second block) what the server received.

```text
== CORS: what page JavaScript could do ==
A simple GET, server sends no CORS header
    -> JS blocked: TypeError: Failed to fetch
B simple GET, server allows any origin (*)
    -> JS can read it: 200 {"public": true}
C POST text/plain to a no-CORS endpoint
    -> JS blocked: TypeError: Failed to fetch
D custom header, server answers preflight
    -> JS can read it: 200 {"token_ok": true}
E custom header, server ignores preflight
    -> JS blocked: TypeError: Failed to fetch
F cookies sent, server allows this origin
    -> JS can read it: 200 {"cookies_seen": ["sid"]}
G cookies sent, server answers with *
    -> JS blocked: TypeError: Failed to fetch
```

```text
== CORS: what the API server actually received ==
GET     /nocors     origin=http://localhost:9100 cookies=[] (received)
GET     /public     origin=http://localhost:9100 cookies=[] (received)
POST    /nocors     origin=http://localhost:9100 cookies=[] (received)
OPTIONS /pref       origin=http://localhost:9100 cookies=[] (preflight answered)
GET     /pref       origin=http://localhost:9100 cookies=[] (real request)
OPTIONS /nocors     origin=http://localhost:9100 cookies=[] (received)
GET     /cred       origin=http://localhost:9100 cookies=["sid"] (received)
GET     /cred-star  origin=http://localhost:9100 cookies=["sid"] (received)
```

What the evidence shows:

- **A and B.** With no CORS header, the browser let the request go out but **refused to hand the response to JavaScript** ("Failed to fetch"). The server log confirms that `GET /nocors` *was received and processed*. With `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` the page could read it. So **CORS restricts reading, not sending**.
- **C. The important one.** A cross-origin `POST` with `Content-Type: text/plain` counts as a **simple request**: no preflight, and the server log shows `POST /nocors` **arrived**. The page could not read the answer, but a state-changing action had already happened. This is exactly why CORS is **not** a CSRF defence.
- **D and E. Preflight.** A custom header such as `X-Token` makes the request "non-simple", so the browser first sends an `OPTIONS` **preflight** asking permission. In D the server answered with `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: x-token` and the real request followed. In E the server ignored the preflight: only `OPTIONS /nocors` appears in the log and the real `GET` was **never sent**. Preflights are a real safety net for non-simple requests, since the dangerous request is held back until the server consents.
- **F and G. Credentials.** To send cookies cross-origin the page must opt in (`credentials: "include"`) **and** the server must reply with a specific origin (not `*`) plus `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`. F worked and the server saw the `sid` cookie. In G the server answered `*` together with credentials, which browsers reject. The page was blocked, but note that the server log shows `GET /cred-star` **with the cookie** was received.

Practical consequences: never reflect the request's `Origin` back blindly or use `*` for authenticated APIs, send `Vary: Origin` when you vary the header (so caches do not mix responses), allow only the methods and headers you need, and **protect state-changing endpoints with real authentication and CSRF defences**, not with CORS.

### Content Security Policy (CSP)

CSP is a response header that tells the browser **which sources of code are allowed on this page**. Its main job is to turn a successful XSS injection into a harmless, blocked script.

```text
== CSP: default-src 'self' ==
inline script ran: false | same-origin script file ran: true
console said: Refused to execute inline script because it violates the following Content Security Policy directive: "default-src 'self'". Either the 'unsafe-inline' keyword, a hash ('sha256-DwqCCwuGrUEakwAxEO45bEUUaZXG2JK2oBSHshmRaPk='), or a nonce ('nonce-...') is required to enable inline execution. Note also that 'script-src' was not explicitly set, so 'default-src' is used as a fallback.
```

```text
== CSP: script-src 'nonce-r4nd0m' ==
script with the matching nonce ran: true | script without a nonce ran: false
```

- With `default-src 'self'`, the **inline** script did not run, while a script loaded from the **same origin** (`/app.js`) did. The browser even tells you why and suggests the three ways to allow an inline script: `'unsafe-inline'` (which defeats the point), a **hash** of the exact script, or a **nonce**.
- With `script-src 'nonce-r4nd0m'`, only the script that carried the matching `nonce` attribute ran. An injected `<script>` tag has no way to know the nonce, so it is blocked. The nonce must be **fresh, unguessable and generated per response** (the fixed `r4nd0m` above is for the demo only).
- Start with the header `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only` to see what *would* be blocked without breaking the site, collect the violation reports, then enforce. Aim for a policy with no `unsafe-inline` and no wildcard hosts, and consider `'strict-dynamic'` with nonces for modern apps. These patterns come from CSP guidance and were not all executed here.
- CSP does not replace output escaping. It is the **second** line of defence.

### Cookies: `HttpOnly`, `SameSite` and `Secure`

The server set four cookies: `sid` (`HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax`), `lax`, `strict`, and `none` (`SameSite=None; Secure`). Then the page itself wrote a fifth (`theme`) with JavaScript.

```text
== Cookies the browser stored for localhost ==
sid: httpOnly=true sameSite=Lax secure=false
lax: httpOnly=false sameSite=Lax secure=false
strict: httpOnly=false sameSite=Strict secure=false
none: httpOnly=false sameSite=None secure=true

document.cookie on the page itself: "lax=1; strict=1; none=1; theme=dark" (the HttpOnly sid is missing; theme was just written by the page's own script)
```

- **`HttpOnly`** hides a cookie from JavaScript: `document.cookie` listed `lax`, `strict`, `none` and `theme` but **not `sid`**. XSS can then no longer *read* the session cookie, but injected code can still *use* the session by making requests from the victim's page, so `HttpOnly` limits damage and does not stop XSS.
- **`Secure`** restricts a cookie to HTTPS (browsers treat `localhost` as a special case, which is why `none` was accepted over plain `http` here; in production, always use HTTPS).
- **`SameSite`** controls whether the cookie is attached to **cross-site** requests. We tested it with an "attacker" page on a different **site** (`127.0.0.1`) that submits a form and has a link to the target on `localhost`:

```text
== Which cookies arrived? (attacker page lives on 127.0.0.1, the target on localhost) ==
cross-site form POST           -> ["none"]
cross-site link click (GET)    -> ["lax","none","sid","theme"]
same-site form POST            -> ["lax","none","sid","strict","theme"]
```

  - **Cross-site form POST:** only the `SameSite=None` cookie was sent. `Lax`, `Strict` and the `Lax` session cookie were **withheld**. This is what blocks classic CSRF.
  - **Cross-site link click (a top-level GET navigation):** `Lax` cookies **were** sent (so clicking a link into a site keeps you logged in) but `Strict` was not.
  - **Same-site POST:** everything was sent, including `Strict`.
- Consequence for design: use **`SameSite=Lax`** for session cookies (the usual default in modern browsers when the attribute is missing, though behaviour for cookies with no explicit attribute has special cases such as a short-lived POST exception that we avoided by setting the attribute explicitly). Use `Strict` for highly sensitive cookies where the usability trade-off is acceptable, and `None; Secure` only when you genuinely need cross-site use (embedded widgets, third-party contexts), knowing that makes the cookie CSRF-able.
- Name cookies with the `__Host-` prefix (`Secure`, `Path=/`, no `Domain`) to stop sibling subdomains from overwriting them; this prefix behaviour is from the cookie specifications and was not tested here.

### Framing and clickjacking

An attacker page can load your page inside an invisible `<iframe>` and trick the user into clicking its buttons ("**clickjacking**"). Two headers prevent it: `X-Frame-Options: DENY` (or `SAMEORIGIN`), and the modern CSP directive `frame-ancestors`. Test result:

```text
== Framing the target page from the attacker page ==
framed page with X-Frame-Options: DENY shows its content: false
framed page without the header shows its content:        true
console said: Refused to display 'http://localhost:9101/' in a frame because it set 'X-Frame-Options' to 'deny'.
```

The page that sent `X-Frame-Options: DENY` was not displayed inside the attacker's frame, and the browser logged why; the page without the header rendered normally.

### Other headers worth knowing (not tested above)

- **`Strict-Transport-Security` (HSTS):** tells browsers to use HTTPS only for your site for a period. Needs HTTPS to be tested and is therefore not exercised here.
- **`X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`:** stops the browser from guessing a file's type (a text file served as script).
- **`Referrer-Policy`:** controls how much of the URL is sent in the `Referer` header to other sites (URLs can contain tokens or private paths).
- **`Permissions-Policy`:** switches off browser features (camera, microphone, geolocation) your site does not use.
- **`Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy` and `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy`:** isolate your pages and resources from other sites' windows and embeds.
- **`Cache-Control: no-store`** on responses with private data.

## Worked example

Goal: your single-page app on `https://app.example.com` calls an API on `https://api.example.com`. A teammate "fixed" a CORS error by sending `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` with credentials enabled in the framework. Review it with this lesson's evidence.

1. **Is it even valid?** `*` combined with credentials is rejected by browsers (test G), so the teammate probably reflected the request `Origin` instead. That is **worse**: any website can now read authenticated responses, because the server says yes to every origin.
2. **Fix the CORS config:** an **allow-list** of exact origins (`https://app.example.com`), `Allow-Credentials: true` only if cookies are needed, `Vary: Origin`, and only the methods and headers required.
3. **Check what CORS never covered:** the API's `POST /transfer` accepts `text/plain` and cookies. Test C shows such a request is *sent* regardless of CORS. Require a JSON `Content-Type` (forcing a preflight), verify the `Origin` header server-side, use `SameSite=Lax` on the session cookie, and add a CSRF token or custom header.
4. **Cookies:** the session cookie must be `HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax` (and use `__Host-` if possible). Both `app` and `api` are the same **site** (`example.com`), so `Lax` cookies still flow between them while cross-site pages are excluded.
5. **Add a CSP** to the app: `default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'nonce-...'; frame-ancestors 'none'`; roll it out in report-only mode first.
6. **Re-test with a real browser** (as this lesson does) so the final configuration is observed, not assumed.

The result: the original error is solved properly, three latent weaknesses are closed, and each statement in the review can be backed with an experiment.

## In real products

- **Frameworks and gateways** set many of these headers for you (for example `helmet` for Express, Django's security middleware, ASP.NET Core's HSTS and CORS middleware, Nginx and CDN rules), but defaults vary, so read your actual response headers in the browser's Network tab.
- **Security scanners** (Mozilla Observatory, securityheaders.com, Lighthouse) grade exactly these headers and cookie flags, and enterprise questionnaires ask for them.
- **Single-page apps with a separate API** live with CORS daily; teams often avoid it with a reverse proxy that serves both under one origin, which also simplifies cookies.
- **Third-party scripts** (analytics, chat, ads) run with full page privileges, which is why large sites invest in strict CSPs, Subresource Integrity (`integrity` attributes on script tags), sandboxed iframes and tag-management governance.
- **Browser changes** over time (third-party cookie restrictions, default `SameSite=Lax`, new isolation features) change behaviour; always retest on current browsers.

> [!pitfall] Believing CORS protects the server. It only controls whether a page can read a cross-origin response. Requests are still sent (tests A, C and G all reached the server), so authentication, authorisation, CSRF protection and input validation are required regardless of CORS.

> [!pitfall] Reflecting the `Origin` header into `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`, or allowing `null` or wildcard subdomains loosely, together with credentials. This tells every website it may read logged-in users' data. Use a fixed allow-list of exact origins.

> [!pitfall] A CSP with `unsafe-inline`, `unsafe-eval` or `https:`/`*` wildcards. It makes the header look present while allowing the injected script it exists to stop. Use nonces or hashes, and treat each wildcard as an exception that needs a reason.

> [!pitfall] Thinking `HttpOnly` fixes XSS or that `SameSite` replaces CSRF tokens everywhere. `HttpOnly` stops cookie theft only; injected code still acts as the user. `SameSite` is a strong layer but has edge cases (same-site attackers on sibling subdomains, older browsers, `None` cookies), so keep tokens or custom headers on state-changing APIs.

> [!tip] Debug in the browser: the Network tab shows the preflight and its response headers, and the Console prints the exact CORS, CSP and framing violation (as in the transcripts above). Reproduce the failing request with curl using the same `Origin` header to see what the server really returns. A CORS error with no `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` in the response is often a server error (404, 500) or a redirect in disguise.

> [!tip] Put the policy in one place (reverse proxy, framework middleware, or your CDN) with an automated test that fetches your pages and asserts the headers, so a deploy cannot silently drop them. Roll CSP out in report-only mode and watch the reports.

> [!cost] Preflights add a full round trip before the real request. Reduce them by using simple requests where safe, setting `Access-Control-Max-Age` so browsers cache the answer (we used 600 seconds; browsers cap this value), or serving the API from the same origin through a proxy. Strict CSPs cost development effort once (removing inline scripts) and then little.

> [!security] Treat browser-enforced headers as defence in depth, not as the primary control: the primary controls are output escaping, authorisation, input validation and least privilege on the server. Do not hold long-lived secrets in `localStorage` (any XSS can read it); prefer short-lived tokens, `HttpOnly` cookies, and rotation. Use HTTPS everywhere with HSTS, keep third-party scripts to a minimum and pin them with Subresource Integrity where possible.

> [!prod] Production checklist: HTTPS with HSTS; session cookies `HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax` (and `__Host-` where possible); exact-origin CORS allow-list with `Vary: Origin`; CSRF tokens or custom-header checks on state-changing endpoints; CSP with nonces (report-only first, then enforced) and `frame-ancestors`; `nosniff`, `Referrer-Policy` and `Permissions-Policy`; an automated header test in CI; monitoring of CSP reports; periodic retesting on current browsers.

